@startuml

salt
{
  <b>PUBLISH CONVENTIONS FROM DATA QUALITY</b>  { Datasets | Coverage | Bundle | Receipt }
  --
  {
    {
      <b>The datasets</b>
      --
      {#
        Code | Name | Artefact type | Catalogue
        <b>ore.conventions</b> | ORE Instrument Conventions | conventions | ORE Analytics
        refdata.currency_pair_conventions | Standard Currency Pair Conventions | currency_pair_conventions | FX Market Conventions
        refdata.currency_pair_convention_calendars | Currency Pair Convention Calendars | ... | FX Market Conventions
      }
      <i>Payload: a curated artefact row per party, seeded in the system tenant.</i>
      --
      <b>What the person cannot read</b>
      <b>No read of an ores_dq_*_artefact_tbl over NATS.</b>
      DQ publishes artefact types and datasets, not the rows they stage.
      What is about to land is readable only in the database.
    } | {
      <b>What the publish covers: 25 of 26 kinds</b>
      --
      {#
        Route | Kinds | Subject
        the 24-kind array | 24 | refdata.v1.ops.publish_conventions_from_dq
        currency pair | 1 | refdata.v1.ops.publish_currency_pair_conventions_from_dq
        <b>tenor</b> | <b>0</b> | (no artefact table, no subject)
      }
      <i>The function loops a 24-kind array and dispatches by name transform.</i>
      <i>ores_dq_&lt;kind&gt;_conventions_artefact_tbl into ores_refdata_&lt;kind&gt;_conventions_tbl.</i>
      <i>A new kind costs one array entry and one artefact table, not a function.</i>
      --
      <b>tenor is the 26th</b>
      No ores_dq_tenor_conventions_artefact_tbl exists, so no function can copy it.
      Its 4 rows are copied from the system tenant by ores_iam_provision_tenant_fn
      when the tenant is created, reason system.new_record, and never refreshed.
    }
  }
  --
  {
    {
      <b>Which bundle, and who it publishes for</b>
      --
      {#
        Dataset | Bundle | Scope | Param
        ore.conventions | <b>party_essentials</b> | once per <b>party</b> | {"party_id": "..."}
        refdata.currency_pair_conventions | <b>base</b> | once per <b>tenant</b> | (none)
      }
      --
      The shell spells it: bundles publish party_essentials, with the party id, and wait.
      That reaches dq.v1.ops.publish_bundle, which starts a workflow instance: one step per dataset.
      The conventions step is refdata.v1.ops.publish_conventions_from_dq.
      [ Publish ] | [ Read the run ]
    } | {
      <b>Receipt: the four counts the step returns</b>
      --
      {#
        The publish | inserted | updated | skipped | deleted
        first party, ore.conventions | <b>2,188</b> | 0 | 0 | 0
        each later party | <b>2,155</b> | 0 | <b>33</b> | 0
        a re-publish of the same party | 0 | 0 | <b>2,188</b> | 0
        currency_pair, second publish | 0 | <b>179</b> | 0 | 0
      }
      --
      <b>The 33 is not a defect</b>
      Four kinds carry no party_id. Two of them are in the 24-kind array:
      ibor_index 10 + overnight_index 23 = 33. The insert is guarded by
      "this key is not already here for this tenant and party", and with no party
      column it finds the row the first party's publish wrote.
      <b>The 33 reads: already present, tenant-wide.</b>
      --
      <b>A re-publish does not carry a corrected curated row</b>
      The 24-kind function has no update path: it inserts where the row is absent
      and counts everything else as skipped. Its result carries no updated count.
      <b>25 of the 26 kinds cannot take a correction. currency_pair is the one that can.</b>
      <i>Correcting a row a tenant already holds is the change journey's job, per row.</i>
    }
  }
}

@enduml
